  "final_text": "All four steps are done. The dashboard, panel title, and alert rule all read back as expected.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has the time field `timestamp`. Response codes are in `response`, a string with a `response.keyword` subfield for aggregations.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-b7449d2b Web logs' (id `s4-b7449d2b-web-logs-75cddc11cba7`) has two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart of count on a `timestamp` date histogram.\n   - \"Requests by response code\": a bar chart of count split by `response.keyword`, top 10 terms.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-b7449d2b 5xx errors' (id `b418d77b-9999-418a-bc9a-18e813c8f9d8`) is a `.es-query` rule in `stackAlerts`, scheduled every 1m. It counts documents with `response.keyword` between \"500\" and \"599\" over a 5-minute window and fires when the count is greater than 10. It was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n4. **Panel edit:** The response-code panel is now titled \"Responses by code\". I replaced it through `update_panel` rather than editing the title in place, so its chart settings were rebuilt from the same spec. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true` (status `ok`).\n\nChoices I made:\n- The 5xx match is a string range on `response.keyword`. That is correct for three-digit codes, but it would also match any longer code that starts with 5xx and sorts in that range. The sample data only has three-digit codes.\n- I chose a bar chart for the response-code panel because the request didn't name a chart type.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
